{
  "strategy": "Identify the dialogue in the clip and visually determine which speaker is the tallest person among the speaking characters, then map that speaker’s utterance to the correct option. Use ASR for the spoken sentences and a bounded visual description pass for speaker identity/relative height rather than broad whole-video guessing.",
  "tool_calls": [
    {
      "step": 1,
      "tool": "asr",
      "arguments": {
        "video_path": "<VIDEO_PATH>",
        "start_time": null,
        "end_time": null
      },
      "purpose": "Get the full transcript with timestamps so the exact spoken sentence matching the answer options can be identified directly from speech.",
      "depends_on": []
    },
    {
      "step": 2,
      "tool": "dense_captioner",
      "arguments": {
        "video_path": "<VIDEO_PATH>",
        "start_time": 0,
        "end_time": 25,
        "granularity": "segment"
      },
      "purpose": "Describe the early dialogue scene with the family/table confrontation so the refiner can identify which visible speaker says each transcript line and compare the speakers’ relative heights.",
      "depends_on": []
    }
  ],
  "refinement_instructions": "Build the trace from scratch. First, use the ASR transcript as the authoritative source for which of the option sentences is actually spoken in the clip; cite the matching transcript text and its timestamped segment(s). Then use the dense_captioner only for the bounded family-confrontation scene to determine which character is speaking during that utterance and whether that speaker is the tallest person among the visible speakers. Do not infer from the video summary alone. Preserve modality separation: ASR grounds the sentence, dense_captioner grounds speaker identity and relative height. If the ASR shows only one option sentence is spoken, and dense_captioner indicates that speaker is the tallest person, update the answer accordingly. If multiple option-like lines appear, align them by timestamps and choose the one spoken by the tallest visible speaker. If dense_captioner is too coarse to distinguish a speaker at a specific utterance timestamp, keep the trace explicit about any remaining uncertainty rather than inventing a match."
}
